example:
```js
const numbers = [10, 2, 1];
numbers.sort((a, b) => a - b);
// [1, 2, 10]
```

For a = 2 and b = 10, a - b is negative, so 2 belongs before 10. The function compares pairs; it does not directly return the whole sorted array.

```js
const result = Promise.resolve(5);
result.then(value => console.log(value));
```

result is a Promise. The callback receives the resolved value, 5. Even for an already fulfilled Promise, the then callback is scheduled rather than called inline at that exact line.

```js
async function load() {
  const response = await fetch("/user");
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error("Request failed");
  return await response.json();
}
```

First wait for a response. Then check its status. Then wait for the body to be parsed. Errors can reject the returned Promise.

```js
console.log("A");
setTimeout(() => console.log("B"), 0);
console.log("C");
// A, C, B
```

The current work prints A and C. The callback prints B only after the current synchronous work has completed.

