if (title) {
  title.textContent = "Welcome";
}
```

The selector finds the first h1 element. The condition handles the possibility that none exists. textContent changes its text without interpreting that text as HTML.

```js
const text = JSON.stringify({ score: 3 });
// text is: '{"score":3}'
const value = JSON.parse(text);
value.score; // 3
```

The text is a string. After parsing, value is an object again. These are different representations.

```css
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
}
```

With the default row direction, the first rule creates a row layout. Space-between spreads items along the row, and align-items centers them on the cross axis.

```css
.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}
```

This example uses two columns when there is room and one column on narrower viewports. The breakpoint should follow the content's needs.

```jsx
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>;
}
// <Greeting name="Ada" /> shows Hello, Ada
```

The braces insert a JavaScript value into the JSX. Greeting can be reused with another name without duplicating its layout.

```jsx
function Counter({ count, onIncrement }) {
  return <button onClick={onIncrement}>{count}</button>;
}
```

The child displays count and calls onIncrement when clicked. The parent decides how to update the value and passes the new count back down.

```jsx
const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);
const increment = () => setCount(c => c + 1);
```

count is the value for the current render. setCount requests an update. The function form receives the previous pending value and computes the next one.

```jsx
React.useEffect(() => {
  const onResize = () => console.log(window.innerWidth);
  window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
  return () => window.removeEventListener("resize", onResize);
}, []);
```

The returned function removes the same listener that was added. It prevents old listeners from being left behind.

```jsx
const inputRef = React.useRef(null);
// <input ref={inputRef} />
// Later, from an appropriate handler:
inputRef.current?.focus();
```

React can place the input element in current. The optional access handles the time when the element is not available.

```jsx
const [name, setName] = React.useState("");
// In the returned JSX:
<input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />
```

Typing triggers onChange. The handler stores the new text. React renders that text as the input's value.

